    <string name="guide_header_title">Why 180 SPM Matters</string>
    <string name="what_is_cadence_title">What is Running Cadence?</string>
    <string name="what_is_cadence_desc">Cadence is the total number of steps you take per minute (SPM) with both feet. Elite distance runners naturally strike at 180+ SPM, which optimizes energy expenditure and minimizes peak impact forces on joints.</string>
    <string name="key_benefits_title">KEY BENEFITS OF HIGHER CADENCE</string>
    <string name="joint_protection_title">Joint &amp; Knee Protection</string>
    <string name="joint_protection_desc">A faster turnover reduces stride length, shifting impact from heel-striking to midfoot landing. This cuts joint load by up to 20%.</string>
    <string name="running_economy_title">Improved Running Economy</string>
    <string name="running_economy_desc">Shorter ground contact time converts spring energy through tendons more efficiently, helping you run farther with less fatigue.</string>
    <string name="injury_prevention_title">Injury Prevention</string>
    <string name="injury_prevention_desc">Over-striding is the #1 cause of shin splints and runner\'s knee. Matching audio pulse syncs your nervous system to safer form.</string>
    <string name="progression_tips_title">Tips for Cadence Progression</string>
    <string name="tip1">Increase target by 5% at a time. If your natural pace is 160 SPM, start with 168 SPM before jumping to 180.</string>
    <string name="tip2">Focus on quick feet, not faster speed. Keep strides light and soft under your hips.</string>
    <string name="tip3">Use rhythm sound effects and vibration feedback on short 10-minute interval runs to build muscle memory.</string>
